text: |-
  1984
  @TheOfficial1984
  0...
  Why would a company, founded by members israeli intelligence, funded
  by Peter Thiel, be moderating content for US election integrity on X?
  Cyabra's Role with X (Formerly Twitter): A
  Detailed Overview
  Cyabra, a Tel Aviv-based cybersecurity firm founded in 2020
  by former IDF Unit 8200 alumni (Ofer Ravid, Avi Koren, and
  Eran Lederman), specializes in Al-driven detection of
  disinformation, bot networks, and inauthentic online
  behavior. The company uses machine learning to analyze
  social media profiles, behavioral patterns, and engagement
  clusters, identifying coordinated campaigns (e.g., bot farms
  amplifying misinformation). With over 40 employees (many
  IDF reservists), Cyabra has been pivotal in X's efforts to
  combat bots and fake accounts since Elon Musk's 2022
  acquisition. Its role evolved from a pre-acquisition
  consultant to an ongoing analytical partner, particularly for
  election integrity and content moderation.
  Cyabra's involvement aligns with Musk's pledge to "defeat
  the spam bots or die trying" (X post, May 2022). While not a
  core operational integration like CHEQ (for real-time bot
  blocking), Cyabra provides research, reporting, and
  insights that inform X's platform policies and advertiser
  trust. No formal contract details are public, but
  collaborations are multi-year (since 2022) and ongoing as of
  2025. Below, I'll break it down by timeline, key contributions,
  and broader context.
  6:15 PM • Nov 3, 2025 • 1,880 Views
  • 4
  {7 16
  © 84
  • 5
  Post your reply
  Reply
  1984 @TheOfficial1984 • 1h
  With Musk and Twitter now expected to
  close the acquisition deal, the public may never get
  a close look at the evidence each side gathered on
  the issue or a court ruling on Musk's claims that bots
  are more pervasive on the platform than it has let on.
  But Cyabra is coming forward now, Brahmy said, after
  the company's work for Musk's team was revealed in
  court filings and hearings in the acquisition dispute.
  The Israel-based company has roughly 40 employees
  and counts the US State Department and various
  consumer brands as customers of its digital
  authenticity measurement tools, he said. Among its
  investors is the Peter Thiel-backed Founders Fund
  1]2
  O 26
  Mr Woody © @MrWoodysGoodies • 1h
